#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""gatbox-sync — push finished rail sessions to the hubs (arcade-tracker, and any other).

One direction only. This reads from gatbox-web on loopback and writes to the hub; the hub
never connects here. Run as a oneshot from gatbox-sync.timer, every couple of minutes.

**It imports nothing from gatboxweb, on purpose.** The obvious shortcut -- import
gatboxweb.report and build the payload in-process -- is worse:

  * The report cache belongs to gatbox-web: its CacheDirectory, keyed on the CSV's size and
    mtime. This job has its own DynamicUser and so its own cache, and would re-run
    gatbox-rail-report over a ten-thousand-sample CSV that gatbox-web had already analysed.
  * Reading over HTTP means the hub stores the very numbers the dashboard shows -- the same
    cached report object -- rather than a second computation that agrees today and could
    drift later.
  * The sandbox gets much tighter: no /var/log/gatbox, no /dev, no matplotlib. One loopback
    socket, one credential, its own state directory.

What it reads from gatbox-web:
    GET /api/rail/sessions?limit=    what exists, and which file is still being written
    GET /api/rail/report/<file>      the whole summary; every contract field comes from here
    GET /api/rail/samples/<file>     the trace, already reduced by gatbox-web's reducer

What it sends, per contract/v1 (vendored at docs/contract/v1/):
    POST /api/v1/ingest              rail_session items, readings nested
    GET  /api/v1/roster              cached for the dashboard and for slug checks
    GET  /api/v1/machines/<s>/orders cached so the 7" can show what is already reported

Idempotency is the hub's, not ours: every uid is derived from the device id and the CSV's
own name, so re-sending changes nothing and comes back "duplicate". **The sent ledger is an
optimisation, not correctness** -- delete state/sent.json and everything is simply re-sent
and acknowledged as duplicate. That is the recovery for "the state looks wrong".

**Exit codes.** systemd marks a oneshot failed on a non-zero exit, and a timer-driven unit
that fails routinely fills `systemctl --failed` with noise that hides the failures that
matter. So non-zero is reserved for "a person must act, and retrying cannot help":

    1   the token is malformed, or the hub refuses it (401/403 on ingest), or the hub
        rejects the request as malformed (400) -- a contract bug on our side
    0   everything else, including no hub reachable, gatbox-web down, 429 or 5xx, and
        items the hub rejected

A portable bench box being off its home network is the normal case, not a fault. What is
happening is still in the journal and in status.json, which is what the dashboard's SYSTEM
panel reads -- that is the channel for "nothing has gone out lately", not a failed unit.

Stdlib only.

Config. One hub or several, each a file of KEY=value lines, root-owned and mode 600, reached
through LoadCredential= so the files themselves never become readable by this job's user:

    /etc/gatbox/hub.conf            the original single hub, named "default" (Phase 8 keeps it
                                    working unchanged; empty means "not configured")
    /etc/gatbox/hubs.d/<name>.conf  one more hub per file; <name> is [a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,31}

    HUB_URLS=https://tracker.example   one or more, space-separated, tried in order
                                       (no LAN entry by default: the hub binds to loopback
                                        in its own container and is reached by its tunnel)
    HUB_TOKEN=gbx_<public_id>.<secret>
    HUB_LABEL=WORKSHOP                 optional: what the 7" calls this hub (default: the name)

**With one hub, nothing is routed**: everything goes to it, exactly as before Phase 8, and the
hub refuses a machine it does not know. With two or more, each item goes to the one hub whose
roster holds its machine. A machine no roster has is *held* -- sent nowhere, retried by itself
once a roster gains it -- and a machine two rosters claim is a *conflict*, sent nowhere until one
of them lets go. Both are listed in status.json for the SYSTEM panel. Every uid is salted with the
destination hub's device id, as the contract says, because each hub issues its own token.

State. The default hub keeps the top level of the state directory, exactly where a single hub has
always kept it (sent.json, roster.json, orders.json), so nothing moves when a second hub is
added. Each hubs.d hub has hubs/<name>/ with the same files. hub.json beside them holds what the
hub last said about itself in /api/v1/health (app, capabilities) and its label: the dashboard
cannot read /etc/gatbox, and the JOB card has to work from cache on a box that is mostly offline.
status.json at the top is the whole run; with one hub its keys are what they always were.

`phase` (as_found | during | as_left) goes only to a hub whose health lists the "jobs"
capability, and only as one of those three. The tracker lists none, so it never sees the field.

Environment (the unit sets CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY and STATE_DIRECTORY; tests set the rest):
    GATBOX_SYNC_CONF      read the default hub's config from this path instead of the credential
    GATBOX_SYNC_CONF_DIR  read hubs.d from this directory (with GATBOX_SYNC_CONF alone: none)
    GATBOX_SYNC_WEB       gatbox-web's base URL (default http://127.0.0.1)
    GATBOX_SYNC_MAX       sessions per run, per hub (default 10)
    GATBOX_SYNC_ONCE      ignore the ledger: send everything eligible (for a forced re-push)
    GATBOX_SYNC_DRYRUN    build payloads and report, but post nothing
"""
import hashlib
import re
import json
import os
import socket
import sys
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request

WEB = os.environ.get("GATBOX_SYNC_WEB", "http://127.0.0.1").rstrip("/")
STATE = os.environ.get("STATE_DIRECTORY", "/var/lib/gatbox-sync").split(":")[0]
CONTRACT = "v1"

# Ten sessions, not ten readings: a session is one bench run, carrying up to MAX_READINGS
# points. Ten of them is at most 20,000 readings, which is exactly the contract's
# per-request ceiling -- so a capped run is always one POST rather than a split one.
MAX_SESSIONS = int(os.environ.get("GATBOX_SYNC_MAX", "10"))
MAX_READINGS = 2000               # contract/v1: readings per session
HEALTH_TIMEOUT = 2.0              # per candidate URL: an unreachable one must be cheap
WEB_TIMEOUT = 120.0               # a cold report over 10k samples is not quick
POST_TIMEOUT = 120.0
RUN_BUDGET = 240.0                # under the unit's RuntimeMaxSec, so we stop ourselves first

# Say what we are. urllib otherwise sends "Python-urllib/3.13", which is a string generic
# bot protection blocks outright -- Cloudflare in front of the hub refused it with a 403
# while the very same request with any other User-Agent got a 200, curl included. Beyond
# getting through, a request that names the program is simply easier to account for in
# someone else's log than one that names the language it happens to be written in.
USER_AGENT = "gatbox-sync/1 (+https://github.com/digitalunconciousness/Gatbox)"

DRYRUN = bool(os.environ.get("GATBOX_SYNC_DRYRUN"))
IGNORE_LEDGER = bool(os.environ.get("GATBOX_SYNC_ONCE"))
# How many refusals of the same item before it stops being offered. Rejections used to be
# retried for ever: most of what could be refused before Phase 6 cleared on its own -- a
# session the hub had not ingested yet -- but an order for a machine the hub's roster does
# not have never clears, and nothing said so. Five is ten minutes at the usual timer, long
# enough that anything genuinely on its way gets through.
MAX_TRIES = int(os.environ.get("GATBOX_SYNC_MAX_TRIES", "5"))
# Offer the parked ones again, once: the way back after fixing the roster at the hub.
UNPARK = bool(os.environ.get("GATBOX_SYNC_UNPARK"))


def log(msg):
    """Unbuffered, so journalctl shows progress rather than a lump at exit."""
    print(msg, flush=True)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# config
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DEFAULT = "default"                       # the name hub.conf's hub goes by
NAME = re.compile(r"[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,31}")      # whole-string: NAME.fullmatch
PHASES = ("as_found", "during", "as_left")  # what a "jobs" hub accepts; anything else is left out


def conf_path():
    if os.environ.get("GATBOX_SYNC_CONF"):
        return os.environ["GATBOX_SYNC_CONF"]
    creds = os.environ.get("CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY")
    if creds:
        return os.path.join(creds, "hub")
    return "/etc/gatbox/hub.conf"


def conf_files():
    """[(name, path)] for hubs.d, in name order.

    Under systemd, LoadCredential=hubs:/etc/gatbox/hubs.d hands each file over as its own
    credential, hubs_<file>. A test that sets GATBOX_SYNC_CONF alone gets none at all, so it can
    never pick up the real /etc/gatbox/hubs.d of the machine it runs on."""
    if os.environ.get("GATBOX_SYNC_CONF_DIR"):
        where, prefix = os.environ["GATBOX_SYNC_CONF_DIR"], ""
    elif os.environ.get("CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY"):
        where, prefix = os.environ["CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY"], "hubs_"
    elif os.environ.get("GATBOX_SYNC_CONF"):
        return []
    else:
        where, prefix = "/etc/gatbox/hubs.d", ""
    try:
        names = sorted(os.listdir(where))
    except OSError:
        return []
    out = []
    for f in names:
        if not f.startswith(prefix):
            continue                  # the "hub" credential, or anything else in there
        base = f[len(prefix):]
        if not base.endswith(".conf"):
            continue                  # an editor's backup, a README: not a hub
        name = base[:-len(".conf")]
        if not NAME.fullmatch(name) or name == DEFAULT:
            log(f"hubs.d/{base}: ignored (the name must be [a-z0-9-], up to 32, "
                f"and not {DEFAULT!r})")
            continue
        out.append((name, os.path.join(where, f)))
    return out


def parse_conf(text):
    conf = {}
    for line in text.splitlines():
        line = line.strip()
        if line and not line.startswith("#") and "=" in line:
            key, _, value = line.partition("=")
            conf[key.strip()] = value.strip()
    return conf


def read_conf():
    """(hubs, refused). Each hub: {name, label, urls, token, dev, dir}.

    Exits 0 -- not 1 -- when there is no hub at all: a Pi that has never been given a token is
    not broken, it just has nothing to do, and a timer that fails every two minutes for the
    life of the box is noise that hides real failures. Exits 1 for a malformed token, which
    only a person can fix."""
    path = conf_path()
    entries, missing, incomplete = [], None, None
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            conf = parse_conf(fh.read())
        if conf:
            entries.append((DEFAULT, path, conf))
        else:
            incomplete = path         # empty: tmpfiles leaves a placeholder; it means "none"
    except OSError as exc:
        missing = (path, exc.strerror)
    for name, p in conf_files():
        try:
            with open(p, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                entries.append((name, p, parse_conf(fh.read())))
        except OSError as exc:
            log(f"{p}: unreadable ({exc.strerror}); skipped")

    hubs, refused, seen = [], [], {}
    for name, p, conf in entries:
        urls = [u.rstrip("/") for u in conf.get("HUB_URLS", "").split() if u]
        token = conf.get("HUB_TOKEN", "")
        if not urls or not token:
            log(f"{p}: HUB_URLS and HUB_TOKEN are both required; nothing to do")
            continue
        label = conf.get("HUB_LABEL", "").strip()[:24] or name.upper()
        dev = device_id(token)
        if dev is None:
            log(f"{p}: HUB_TOKEN is not of the form gbx_<public_id>.<secret>")
            # fatal: only a person can fix it, so the unit fails -- but the other hubs still run
            refused.append({"name": name, "label": label, "reason": "malformed token",
                            "fatal": True})
            continue
        if dev in seen:
            # One token in two files: the same hub twice. Both rosters would be the same and
            # every machine would be a conflict, so the second is refused, and said so.
            why = f"same device token as {seen[dev]!r}"
            log(f"hub {name!r}: refused, {why}")
            refused.append({"name": name, "label": label, "reason": why})
            continue
        seen[dev] = name
        hubs.append({"name": name, "label": label, "urls": urls, "token": token, "dev": dev,
                     "dir": STATE if name == DEFAULT else os.path.join(STATE, "hubs", name)})
    if not hubs and any(r.get("fatal") for r in refused):
        sys.exit(1)
    if not hubs and not refused:
        if missing and not entries:
            log(f"no hub config at {missing[0]} ({missing[1]}); nothing to do")
        elif incomplete and not entries:
            log(f"{incomplete}: HUB_URLS and HUB_TOKEN are both required; nothing to do")
        sys.exit(0)
    return hubs, refused


def device_id(token):
    """The public id half of gbx_<public_id>.<secret>: what every uid is salted with. None when
    the token is not of that form."""
    if not token.startswith("gbx_") or "." not in token:
        return None
    return token[len("gbx_"):].partition(".")[0]


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# state
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def load(name, default, where=None):
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(where or STATE, name), encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            return json.load(fh)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return default


def save(name, obj, where=None):
    """Atomic: a run killed mid-write must not leave a half-parsed ledger behind."""
    where = where or STATE
    os.makedirs(where, exist_ok=True)
    path = os.path.join(where, name)
    tmp = path + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        json.dump(obj, fh, indent=1, sort_keys=True)
    os.replace(tmp, path)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# http
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def get_json(url, timeout, token=None):
    req = urllib.request.Request(url, method="GET")
    req.add_header("User-Agent", USER_AGENT)
    if token:
        req.add_header("Authorization", f"Bearer {token}")
    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:
        return json.loads(r.read().decode("utf-8"))


def post_json(url, body, token, timeout=POST_TIMEOUT):
    """(status, parsed body). An HTTP error still carries a contract {"error": …}, which is
    worth reading rather than discarding as an exception."""
    data = json.dumps(body, allow_nan=False).encode("utf-8")
    req = urllib.request.Request(url, data=data, method="POST")
    req.add_header("User-Agent", USER_AGENT)
    req.add_header("Content-Type", "application/json")
    req.add_header("Authorization", f"Bearer {token}")
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as r:
            return r.status, json.loads(r.read().decode("utf-8"))
    except urllib.error.HTTPError as exc:
        raw = exc.read().decode("utf-8", "replace")
        try:
            return exc.code, json.loads(raw)
        except ValueError:
            return exc.code, {"error": raw.strip()[:200] or exc.reason}


def pick_hub(urls, token):
    """(url, health body) for the first URL whose /api/v1/health answers, or (None, None). Chosen per run and deliberately not
    remembered: GATBOX moves between its own hotspot and the shop's Wi-Fi, and a remembered
    address is wrong exactly when it matters. An unreachable entry costs one failed connect,
    so listing a LAN address and the tunnel together is safe at either site."""
    for url in urls:
        try:
            body = get_json(f"{url}/api/v1/health", HEALTH_TIMEOUT)
        except urllib.error.HTTPError as exc:
            # Something answered and refused, which is a different problem from a dead
            # network and wants saying differently. /api/v1/health takes no token and the
            # application cannot produce a 401 or 403 for it, so one of those means
            # something in front of the hub -- a tunnel, a proxy, a WAF -- is doing it.
            note = ""
            if exc.code in (401, 403):
                note = ("; health takes no token, so this is something in front of the hub, "
                        "not the hub")
            log(f"  {url}: refused (HTTP {exc.code} {exc.reason}){note}")
            continue
        except (urllib.error.URLError, OSError, ValueError, socket.timeout) as exc:
            log(f"  {url}: unreachable ({exc})")
            continue
        if body.get("contract") != CONTRACT:
            log(f"  {url}: speaks contract {body.get('contract')!r}, not {CONTRACT!r}")
            continue
        log(f"  {url}: ok")
        return url, body
    return None, None


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# building a payload
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def uid_session(dev, file_name):
    return hashlib.sha256(f"{dev}|rail_session|{file_name}".encode()).hexdigest()[:32]


def uid_reading(dev, file_name, epoch):
    # The .3f is contractual. At .6f the same reading hashes differently and the trace is
    # stored twice, because the hub's duplicate check is on this exact string.
    return hashlib.sha256(
        f"{dev}|reading|{file_name}|{epoch:.3f}".encode()
    ).hexdigest()[:32]


def epoch_of(iso):
    """The report's ISO strings are the CSV's: local wall clock, no offset. Only used to
    order sessions for sending; the contract carries epochs, which come from the samples."""
    try:
        return time.mktime(time.strptime(iso[:19], "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S"))
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return 0.0


def build_item(dev, file_name, report, samples, jobs=False):
    """One rail_session item, contract/v1. Every number comes from the report as-is: the hub
    stores and does not recompute, so anything derived here would be a second opinion."""
    fields = samples.get("fields") or []
    idx = {name: i for i, name in enumerate(fields)}
    readings = []
    for row in samples.get("rows") or []:
        epoch = row[idx["epoch"]]
        raw = row[idx["raw"]] if "raw" in idx else None
        readings.append({
            "uid": uid_reading(dev, file_name, epoch),
            "epoch": epoch,
            "up": row[idx["up"]] if "up" in idx else None,
            "v": row[idx["v"]] if "v" in idx else None,
            "raw": raw,
            "unit": row[idx["unit"]] if "unit" in idx else None,
            "mode": row[idx["mode"]] if "mode" in idx else None,
            "alarm": row[idx["alarm"]] if "alarm" in idx else None,
            # The compact row has no ol field. raw is the precise signal -- v being null
            # also means "no value here", which is not the same thing.
            "ol": raw in ("inf", "-inf"),
        })

    session = report.get("session") or {}
    profile = report.get("profile") or {}
    clock = report.get("clock") or {}
    powered = report.get("powered") or {}
    window = report.get("window")
    verdict = report.get("verdict")
    over = report.get("over_voltage") or []

    item = {
        "kind": "rail_session",
        "uid": uid_session(dev, file_name),
        "file": file_name,
        "machine": report.get("machine"),
        "samples": session.get("samples"),
        "rate": session.get("rate"),
        "duration_s": session.get("duration_s"),
        "timebase": session.get("timebase"),
        "clock": {"source": clock.get("source"),
                  "ntp_from": epoch_of(clock.get("ntp_from")) or None},
        "profile": {"id": profile.get("id"), "label": profile.get("label"),
                    "kind": profile.get("kind"), "rail": profile.get("rail")},
        "mode": report.get("mode_key"),
        "window": ({"lo": window.get("lo"), "hi": window.get("hi"),
                    "unit": window.get("unit"), "source": window.get("source")}
                   if window else None),
        "alarm_hi": report.get("alarm_hi"),
        "powered": ({"mean": powered.get("mean"), "min": powered.get("min"),
                     "max": powered.get("max"), "readings": powered.get("readings"),
                     "in_window_pct": powered.get("in_window_pct")}
                    if powered else None),
        "verdict": ({"state": verdict.get("state"), "detail": verdict.get("detail"),
                     "suspect": verdict.get("suspect")} if verdict else None),
        "counts": {
            "over_voltage": len(over),
            "excursions": len(report.get("excursions") or []),
            "power_cycles": len(report.get("power_cycles") or []),
            "gaps": len(report.get("gaps") or []),
            "ol_events": len(report.get("ol_events") or []),
            "marks": len(report.get("marks") or []),
            "suspect": sum(1 for o in over if o.get("suspect")),
        },
        "readings": readings,
    }
    # The first and last reading's epochs, not the report's local ISO strings: the contract
    # wants epochs and these are the only unambiguous ones we have.
    if readings:
        item["started"] = readings[0]["epoch"]
        item["ended"] = readings[-1]["epoch"]
    # Optional, and only for a hub that said it takes it: anything else rejects the item there.
    if jobs and report.get("phase") in PHASES:
        item["phase"] = report["phase"]
    return item


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# the run
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def ledger(raw):
    """The sent ledger in its two-namespace form, migrating the old flat one.

    It used to be {file: when}. Phase 6 added outbox entries, whose ids are not file names.
    Reading an old file as "all of it is session files" costs nothing; treating it as empty
    would re-send every reading on the Pi to be told "duplicate", which for ten sessions is
    ten thousand rows.
    """
    if not isinstance(raw, dict):
        return {"files": {}, "outbox": {}}
    if "files" in raw or "outbox" in raw:
        d = {"files": dict(raw.get("files") or {}),
             "outbox": dict(raw.get("outbox") or {}),
             "tries": dict(raw.get("tries") or {}),
             "parked": dict(raw.get("parked") or {})}
    else:
        d = {"files": dict(raw), "outbox": {}, "tries": {}, "parked": {}}
    if UNPARK and d["parked"]:
        log(f"  releasing {len(d['parked'])} parked item(s) to be tried again")
        d["parked"], d["tries"] = {}, {}
    return d


def outbox_items(dev, web_entries, sent, jobs=False):
    """gatbox-web's outbox, as contract items. Never the entries already acknowledged.

    This is the one place that can turn an entry's `session_file` into a session uid: the
    rule is sha256(f"{device}|rail_session|{file}") and gatbox-web cannot read the device id,
    which comes from the token this process holds through LoadCredential.
    """
    items = []
    for e in web_entries:
        uid = e.get("uid")
        kind = e.get("kind")
        if not uid or uid in sent["outbox"] or uid in sent["parked"]:
            continue
        if kind == "order":
            item = {"kind": "order", "uid": uid, "machine": e.get("machine"),
                    "issue": e.get("issue"), "priority": e.get("priority") or "Medium",
                    "created": e.get("created"), "technician": "bench"}
            if e.get("session_file"):
                item["rail_session"] = uid_session(dev, e["session_file"])
            # Omitted rather than sent as null when nothing is attached: the hub treats a
            # present rail_session as a link it must resolve, and null is not a link.
            items.append(item)
        elif kind == "session_tag":
            item = {"kind": "session_tag", "uid": uid, "order": e.get("order"),
                    "rail_session": uid_session(dev, e.get("session_file") or ""),
                    "created": e.get("created")}
            if e.get("note"):
                item["note"] = e["note"]
            if jobs and e.get("phase") in PHASES:
                item["phase"] = e["phase"]
            items.append(item)
    return items


def eligible(sessions, sent):
    """Finished sessions the hub has not acknowledged, oldest first.

    Never the live file: the contract forbids it, and for a reason that is easy to miss --
    a partial session reduces into different buckets than the finished one, so its readings
    would hash differently and the duplicate check would not catch the overlap."""
    out = []
    for s in sessions:
        name = s.get("file")
        if not name or s.get("live"):
            continue
        if s.get("error"):
            continue                      # a report that would not run has nothing to send
        if not s.get("machine"):
            continue                      # no machine: the hub would reject it as unknown
        if not IGNORE_LEDGER and name in sent:
            continue
        out.append(name)
    return sorted(out)                    # rail_YYYYmmdd_HHMMSS sorts chronologically


def hub_info(h, body):
    """hub.json: what the hub last said about itself, kept when it cannot be reached."""
    info = load("hub.json", {}, h["dir"])
    info = info if isinstance(info, dict) else {}
    info.update({"name": h["name"], "label": h["label"], "at": time.time()})
    if body is not None:
        caps = body.get("capabilities")
        info.update({"app": body.get("app"), "contract": body.get("contract"),
                     "capabilities": [c for c in caps if isinstance(c, str)]
                     if isinstance(caps, list) else [],
                     "url": h["url"], "reached_at": time.time()})
    info.setdefault("capabilities", [])
    save("hub.json", info, h["dir"])
    return info


def roster_slugs(h):
    machines = load("roster.json", {}, h["dir"]).get("machines") or []
    return {m["slug"] for m in machines if isinstance(m, dict) and m.get("slug")}


def main():
    started = time.monotonic()
    hubs, refused = read_conf()
    rc = 1 if any(r.get("fatal") for r in refused) else 0
    multi = len(hubs) > 1

    for h in hubs:
        name = "" if not multi else f"hub {h['name']} ({h['label']}), "
        log(f"gatbox-sync: {name}device {h['dev']}, web {WEB}")
    try:
        listing = get_json(f"{WEB}/api/rail/sessions?limit=1000", WEB_TIMEOUT)
    except (urllib.error.URLError, OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        log(f"gatbox-web unreachable: {exc}")
        save("status.json", {"at": time.time(), "ok": False,
                             "error": f"gatbox-web unreachable: {exc}", "refused": refused})
        return rc          # it is Restart=always; the next timer will find it
    sessions = listing.get("sessions") or []

    try:
        outbox = (get_json(f"{WEB}/api/orders", WEB_TIMEOUT).get("orders") or [])
    except (urllib.error.URLError, OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        # Not fatal: sessions are the main job and an order waits for the next run.
        log(f"  outbox unreadable ({exc}); sessions only this run")
        outbox = []
    try:
        # The machine on the 7" right now: its open orders are cached even if this Pi has
        # never metered it, which is the usual case for a machine that just came in.
        current = get_json(f"{WEB}/api/machine", WEB_TIMEOUT).get("slug")
    except (urllib.error.URLError, OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
        current = None

    for h in hubs:
        if multi:
            log(f"  {h['label']}:")
        h["url"], body = pick_hub(h["urls"], h["token"])
        h["info"] = hub_info(h, body)
        h["jobs"] = "jobs" in (h["info"].get("capabilities") or [])

    # Routing. One hub takes everything, exactly as before there could be two: the hub itself
    # refuses a machine it does not have, and parking deals with that. With several, the
    # cached rosters decide, and a stale roster is still better than none for a box that is
    # usually offline.
    held, conflicts, waiting = [], {}, []
    if multi:
        for h in hubs:
            if h["url"]:
                refresh_roster(h)
        owners = {}
        for h in hubs:
            for slug in roster_slugs(h):
                owners.setdefault(slug, []).append(h)
        # A machine missing from a roster only means "not that hub's" once that roster has been seen. Until every hub
        # has answered once, a machine one roster lists may be on the unseen one too -- a conflict nobody can see --
        # so nothing goes anywhere: a trace sent to the wrong hub cannot be taken back.
        waiting = [h["label"] for h in hubs if not os.path.exists(os.path.join(h["dir"], "roster.json"))]
        if waiting:
            log(f"  holding everything: no roster yet from {', '.join(waiting)}")

    def route(slug, what):
        if not multi:
            return hubs[0]
        if waiting:
            held.append({"what": what, "machine": slug, "why": "waiting for a roster"})
            return None
        who = owners.get(slug) or []
        if len(who) == 1:
            return who[0]
        if not who:
            held.append({"what": what, "machine": slug})
        else:
            conflicts[slug] = [h["label"] for h in who]
        return None

    by_file = {s.get("file"): s.get("machine") for s in sessions if s.get("file")}
    mine = {h["name"]: {"sessions": [], "outbox": []} for h in hubs}
    for sess in sessions:
        if sess.get("machine") and sess.get("file") and not sess.get("live") \
                and not sess.get("error"):
            h = route(sess["machine"], sess["file"])
            if h:
                mine[h["name"]]["sessions"].append(sess)
    for e in outbox:
        # A tag names its order's machine from Phase 8 on; an older one, the session's.
        slug = e.get("machine") or by_file.get(e.get("session_file"))
        h = route(slug, e.get("kind") or "item") if slug or not multi else None
        if h:
            mine[h["name"]]["outbox"].append(e)
        elif multi and not slug:
            held.append({"what": e.get("kind") or "item", "machine": None})
    if held and not waiting:
        log(f"  held, no hub has the machine: "
            f"{', '.join(sorted({str(x['machine']) for x in held}))}")
    for slug, labels in conflicts.items():
        log(f"  conflict: {slug} is on {' and '.join(labels)}; sent to neither")

    statuses = []
    for h in hubs:
        if multi:
            log(f"  {h['label']}:")
        st, h_rc = run_hub(h, sessions, mine[h["name"]], outbox, current, started, multi)
        rc = max(rc, h_rc)
        if st is not None:
            statuses.append(st)
    if DRYRUN:
        return rc
    save("status.json", combine(hubs, statuses, held, conflicts, refused, waiting))
    return rc


def combine(hubs, statuses, held, conflicts, refused, waiting=()):
    """status.json. With one hub, that hub's own keys as they always were; with several, the
    sums the old tile can show, plus "hubs" for the tile per hub."""
    if len(statuses) == 1:
        out = dict(statuses[0])
    else:
        oks = [s for s in statuses if s.get("ok")]
        out = {"at": time.time(), "ok": bool(statuses) and len(oks) == len(statuses),
               "hub": oks[0].get("hub") if oks else None,
               "queued": sum(s.get("queued") or 0 for s in statuses),
               "sent_total": sum(s.get("sent_total") or 0 for s in statuses),
               "parked": [dict(p, hub=s["label"]) for s in statuses
                          for p in s.get("parked") or []]}
        for k in ("last_created", "last_duplicate", "last_rejected"):
            if any(s.get(k) is not None for s in statuses):
                out[k] = sum(s.get(k) or 0 for s in statuses)
        errs = [f"{s['label']}: {s['error']}" for s in statuses if s.get("error")]
        if errs:
            out["error"] = "; ".join(errs)
    out["hubs"] = statuses
    out["held"] = held
    out["conflicts"] = [{"machine": k, "hubs": v} for k, v in sorted(conflicts.items())]
    out["refused"] = refused
    out["waiting"] = list(waiting)
    return out


def run_hub(h, sessions, mine, outbox, current, started, multi):
    """One hub's share of the run: what it always was for the only hub. Returns (status, rc)."""
    dev, token, where = h["dev"], h["token"], h["dir"]
    base = {"name": h["name"], "label": h["label"], "app": h["info"].get("app"),
            "capabilities": h["info"].get("capabilities") or []}
    sent = ledger({} if IGNORE_LEDGER else load("sent.json", {}, where))
    # Prune the session half against what still exists, so it cannot grow for ever as old
    # CSVs go. **Only that half.** Outbox uids are not file names, and a single flat ledger
    # dropped every one of them here -- re-sending for ever, harmless to the hub because it
    # dedupes, but nothing could ever be reported as sent.
    present = {s.get("file") for s in sessions}
    sent["files"] = {k: v for k, v in sent["files"].items() if k in present}

    queue = [n for n in eligible(mine["sessions"], sent["files"])
             if uid_session(dev, n) not in sent["parked"]]
    log(f"  {len(sessions)} sessions on the Pi, {len(queue)} to send")

    pending = outbox_items(dev, mine["outbox"], sent, h["jobs"])
    # Forget the counters for anything no longer on offer, so neither map grows for ever.
    known = ({uid_session(dev, s["file"]) for s in sessions if s.get("file")}
             | {e["uid"] for e in outbox if e.get("uid")})
    sent["tries"] = {k: v for k, v in sent["tries"].items() if k in known}
    sent["parked"] = {k: v for k, v in sent["parked"].items() if k in known}
    if sent["parked"]:
        log(f"  {len(sent['parked'])} item(s) parked after {MAX_TRIES} refusals; "
            "GATBOX_SYNC_UNPARK=1 offers them again")
    if pending:
        log(f"  {len(pending)} order item(s) queued")

    hub = h["url"]
    parked = [{"what": v.get("what"), "reason": v.get("reason")}
              for v in sent["parked"].values()]
    if not queue and not pending:
        # Still refresh the caches: the roster and open orders are what the dashboard and the
        # 7" read, and they go stale whether or not there is anything to push.
        status = dict(base, at=time.time(), ok=bool(hub), hub=hub, queued=0,
                      sent_total=len(sent["files"]) + len(sent["outbox"]),
                      # Listed here too: the run that has nothing to send is exactly the run
                      # after something was parked, and that is when someone goes looking.
                      parked=parked)
        if hub:
            refresh_caches(h, mine["sessions"], current, status, multi)
        save("sent.json", sent, where)
        if not hub:
            status["error"] = "no hub reachable"
        # 0 either way: out of reach is the normal case for a box that travels (a5a723b's policy, which this path
        # had missed -- it failed the unit every 2 minutes whenever nothing was waiting and no hub answered).
        return status, 0

    if hub is None:
        log(f"  no hub reachable; {len(queue)} queued, nothing sent")
        save("sent.json", sent, where)
        # off the network is the normal case for a box that travels
        return dict(base, at=time.time(), ok=False, queued=len(queue),
                    error="no hub reachable", parked=parked), 0

    batch, skipped = [], 0
    for name in queue[:MAX_SESSIONS]:
        if time.monotonic() - started > RUN_BUDGET:
            log("  run budget spent; the rest waits for the next timer")
            break
        try:
            report = get_json(f"{WEB}/api/rail/report/{urllib.parse.quote(name)}",
                              WEB_TIMEOUT)
            samples = get_json(
                f"{WEB}/api/rail/samples/{urllib.parse.quote(name)}?max={MAX_READINGS}",
                WEB_TIMEOUT)
        except (urllib.error.URLError, OSError, ValueError) as exc:
            log(f"  {name}: could not read from gatbox-web ({exc})")
            skipped += 1
            continue
        if report.get("error"):
            log(f"  {name}: report error, skipped ({report['error']})")
            skipped += 1
            continue
        batch.append(build_item(dev, name, report, samples, h["jobs"]))

    # After the sessions, always. An order naming a session the hub has not ingested is
    # rejected, so putting both in one request is what lets a session and the order attached
    # to it land together instead of the order waiting a run.
    batch.extend(pending)

    if not batch:
        log("  nothing could be built from the queue; see the lines above")
        save("sent.json", sent, where)
        # visible in the journal and the panel; failing every 2 min is not
        return dict(base, at=time.time(), ok=False, hub=hub, queued=len(queue),
                    error="nothing could be built", parked=parked), 0

    total_readings = sum(len(i.get("readings") or []) for i in batch)
    log(f"  posting {len(batch)} session(s), {total_readings} readings")
    if DRYRUN:
        log("  dry run: posting nothing")
        return None, 0

    status_code, body = post_json(f"{hub}/api/v1/ingest",
                                  {"contract": CONTRACT, "device": dev, "items": batch},
                                  token)
    if status_code != 200:
        log(f"  ingest failed: HTTP {status_code} {body.get('error')}")
        save("sent.json", sent, where)
        status = dict(base, at=time.time(), ok=False, hub=hub, queued=len(queue),
                      error=f"HTTP {status_code}: {body.get('error')}", parked=parked)
        # 401/403: the token is wrong, disabled or revoked. 400: we are sending something
        # the hub will never accept, which is a contract bug here. Both need a person, and
        # no amount of retrying helps -- so these are the cases worth failing the unit for.
        # 429 and 5xx are the hub's problem and will clear on their own.
        return status, 1 if status_code in (400, 401, 403) else 0

    now = time.time()
    created = duplicate = rejected = 0
    by_uid = {i["uid"]: i for i in batch}
    for result in body.get("results") or []:
        item = by_uid.get(result.get("uid")) or {}
        name = item.get("file")
        state = result.get("status")
        if state in ("created", "duplicate"):
            # Both mean the hub has it. Only then does the ledger record it -- a rejected
            # item must stay queued so a fixed roster lets the retry through.
            if name:
                sent["files"][name] = now
            elif item.get("uid"):
                sent["outbox"][item["uid"]] = now
            sent["tries"].pop(result.get("uid"), None)
            created += state == "created"
            duplicate += state == "duplicate"
        else:
            rejected += 1
            uid = result.get("uid")
            reason = result.get("reason")
            log(f"  rejected {name or uid}: {reason}")
            if uid:
                n = sent["tries"].get(uid, 0) + 1
                sent["tries"][uid] = n
                if n >= MAX_TRIES:
                    # It is not going to be accepted by being asked again. Keep the reason:
                    # it is the only thing that tells anyone what to fix.
                    sent["parked"][uid] = {"n": n, "reason": reason,
                                           "what": name or item.get("kind") or "item",
                                           "at": now}
                    sent["tries"].pop(uid, None)
                    log(f"  parked {name or uid} after {n} refusals: {reason}")

    log(f"  {created} created, {duplicate} duplicate, {rejected} rejected")
    status = dict(base, at=now, ok=rejected == 0, hub=hub,
                  queued=max(len(queue) - created - duplicate, 0),
                  # The panel reads status.json alone, so the running total goes in here
                  # rather than making it open the ledger as well.
                  sent_total=len(sent["files"]) + len(sent["outbox"]),
                  last_created=created, last_duplicate=duplicate,
                  last_rejected=rejected, skipped=skipped,
                  # The panel reads this alone, so parked items are listed here rather than
                  # left for someone to find in the journal.
                  parked=[{"what": v.get("what"), "reason": v.get("reason")}
                          for v in sent["parked"].values()])
    refresh_caches(h, mine["sessions"], current, status, multi)
    save("sent.json", sent, where)
    if rejected:
        log(f"  {rejected} item(s) rejected and left queued; they will be retried")
    # Not a unit failure: the reasons are above and in status.json, the items stay queued,
    # and a unit that fails every two minutes until someone edits the roster is the noise
    # this policy exists to avoid.
    return status, 0


def refresh_roster(h):
    try:
        roster = get_json(f"{h['url']}/api/v1/roster", WEB_TIMEOUT, h["token"])
    except (urllib.error.URLError, OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        return str(exc)
    save("roster.json", {"at": time.time(), "machines": roster.get("machines") or []},
         h["dir"])
    h["roster_done"] = True
    return None


def refresh_caches(h, sessions, current, status, multi):
    """The roster, and open orders for the machines this Pi has metered for this hub, plus
    the one on the 7" now if this hub has it.

    Orders are fetched per machine rather than wholesale because the hub offers no bulk
    endpoint, so this is deliberately bounded -- not every machine on the floor."""
    if not h.get("roster_done"):
        err = refresh_roster(h)
        if err:
            status["roster_error"] = err
            return

    slugs = sorted({s["machine"] for s in sessions if s.get("machine")})[:40]
    if current and current not in slugs and current in roster_slugs(h):
        slugs.append(current)
    orders, errors = {}, 0
    for slug in slugs:
        try:
            body = get_json(
                f"{h['url']}/api/v1/machines/{urllib.parse.quote(slug)}/orders?status=open",
                WEB_TIMEOUT, h["token"])
            orders[slug] = body.get("orders") or []
        except (urllib.error.URLError, OSError, ValueError):
            errors += 1
    save("orders.json", {"at": time.time(), "open": orders}, h["dir"])
    if errors:
        status["orders_errors"] = errors


